=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is fully operational. Every tested path shows zero packet loss, both VLAN 5 (10.5.0.254) and VLAN 6 (10.6.0.254) gateways on sshswitch respond to ICMP from their respective hosts, OSPF is completely converged across all nine routers with 17 routes apiece, and every host has a valid default route pointing to its gateway.

The Phase 4 broad search found nothing implicated. The infra_sweep reported zero ACL rules, zero static ARP anomalies, and all 15 hosts with IPs and default routes. The l2_snapshot's "duplicate MAC" flags cover only Linux kernel tunnel pseudo-interfaces (tunl0, gre0, etc.) that are uniformly DOWN on every host — a known false positive. Pressure sweep is clean across all 28 devices. DHCP/link history shows zero suspicious events; the carrier counters on switch4.eth0 and sshswitch.eth0 are at zero (startup-only baseline), and sshswitch's journal is empty — there is no trace of a link flap, DHCP disruption, or daemon fault around the reported maintenance window.

The one notable but harmless observation is that sshswitch.eth0 (the physical link to switch4) is a member of bridge br_v1 (55.5.1.0/24, the WAN transit bridge shared with router17). This extends the WAN L2 segment to switch4's native VLAN1, but since switch4's VLAN1 bridge carries no host traffic (all access ports are in br_vlan5 or br_vlan6), no real traffic is affected. VLAN5 and VLAN6 traffic correctly traverses 802.1q sub-interfaces eth0.5/eth0.6 into br_v5/br_v6.

Root cause: no fault is present. The helpdesk report describes a transient disruption during maintenance that has left no mechanism or historical trace — it cannot be confirmed with current evidence.

Fix: no corrective action is required; the network is operating normally.
=== END ===